<h1>Bag Loading Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Bag loading conveyors position, orient, and feed filled bags, pillow bags, stand-up pouches, gusseted bags, and flat pouches, into case packers, tray loaders, and cartoning machines. Bags are flexible products that do not stand upright reliably, change shape depending on fill level, and have variable friction surfaces that make them difficult to convey with the precision required by downstream machines. Bag loading systems use vacuum-assist belts, shingling sections, stacking mechanisms, and servo-controlled feeding to present bags to packing machines in the correct orientation, quantity, and timing across <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food</a>, snack, pet food, pharmaceutical, and consumer goods packaging lines.</p>
<h2 id="challenge-heading">Flat Bag Conveying Challenges</h2>
<p>Bags present conveying problems that rigid products never create. A carton has fixed dimensions, a flat bottom, a predictable center of gravity, and consistent friction with belt surfaces. A filled bag has none of these properties. The bag conforms to the belt surface. Its dimensions change as the contents settle, shift, or compress. Its center of gravity moves as product redistributes inside the bag. Its friction coefficient varies with the printing, lamination, and film type on the bag surface.</p>
<p>Pillow bags (horizontal form-fill-seal bags with fin seals on top and end seals on each side) are the most common flexible package and the most common bag loading challenge. A filled pillow bag lies flat on a conveyor with a rounded top profile and sealed edges that curl upward. The bag slides easily on smooth belts because the contact area is small (only the center bottom contacts the belt; the sealed edges lift off). Speed changes cause pillow bags to slide, rotate, or shift out of alignment.</p>
<p>Stand-up pouches (SUPs) have a bottom gusset that allows them to stand upright, but conveying them upright is unreliable at speed. The filled pouch has a high center of gravity relative to its narrow base width. Accelerating, decelerating, or turning causes pouches to tip, fall, and jam. Most bag loading systems convey stand-up pouches lying flat (on their side or face) rather than upright, standing them up only at the final loading point if the case pack pattern requires upright orientation.</p>
<h3>Product Settling and Dimension Variation</h3>
<p>Filled bags change dimension. A gas-flushed snack bag leaves the bagger taut, and its height and footprint can shift as product settles and the bag is handled. A bag filled with granular product (rice, coffee beans, pet food kibble) has a different shape depending on whether the product has settled to the bottom or is distributed throughout the bag. Bag loading conveyors must tolerate this variation, side guides, stacking mechanisms, and case packer infeed dimensions need clearance for the largest bag condition while still controlling the smallest.</p>
<h2 id="vacuum-heading">Vacuum-Assist Bag Feeding</h2>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">Vacuum-assist conveyors</a> apply suction through perforated belts to hold bags flat against the belt surface during conveying. A vacuum plenum (chamber) beneath the belt creates negative pressure that pulls air through holes in the belt. The suction holds the bag against the belt, preventing sliding during acceleration and deceleration, holding bag edges flat instead of curled, and stabilizing lightweight bags on inclines. mk North America, for example, describes its <a href="https://www.mknorthamerica.com/Products/custom-conveyors/vacuum-belt-conveyor-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">vacuum belt conveyors</a> as drawing air through holes in the belt and frame to hold "light or flat parts against the belt" and to assist with elevation changes.</p>
<p>Vacuum belt systems use <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">modular plastic belts</a> with molded holes or perforated flat belts (polyurethane or silicone with laser-cut holes) running over a vacuum plenum. The plenum connects to a regenerative blower or vacuum pump. Required vacuum level depends on bag weight, film surface, belt open area, and how hard the bag is accelerated. Heavier bags and steeper inclines need more hold-down. Ask the supplier to size the blower for your heaviest bag at your fastest speed change.</p>
<h3>Selective Vacuum Zones</h3>
<p>Advanced vacuum conveyors use zoned vacuum plenums that activate suction only under the section of belt carrying a product. Zones without product have vacuum blocked by a sliding valve or shutoff plate. Zoned vacuum reduces air consumption and blower energy, because open belt holes with no product on them leak suction. Zone control also allows different vacuum levels for different products on the same conveyor (heavier bags in one zone, lighter bags in another).</p>
<p>Vacuum-assist is essential for bag loading systems that use speed changes for metering or gap creation. Belt friction alone often cannot hold a lightweight pouch through the sharp speed increases used for gap creation. Vacuum hold-down maintains bag position through aggressive acceleration and deceleration profiles that would launch an unrestrained bag off the belt surface.</p>
<h2 id="shingle-heading">Shingling &amp; Overlapping</h2>
<p>Shingling overlaps bags like roofing shingles, each bag partially covers the trailing edge of the bag ahead. A speed differential between a faster upstream belt and a slower downstream belt causes trailing bags to ride up onto the leading bag. The overlap depends on the speed ratio and the incoming bag spacing: a larger speed reduction or tighter incoming spacing produces more overlap.</p>
<p>Shingling serves two purposes. First, it compresses the product stream, overlapped bags take less linear space than spaced bags, allowing more bags to accumulate on a given conveyor length. Second, shingling pre-stages bags for case packing patterns that require shingled or nested bag arrangements inside the case. Some case packing patterns load bags vertically (standing on edge) by first shingling them horizontally and then rotating the shingled stack 90 degrees for insertion into the case.</p>
<h3>Controlling Shingle Quality</h3>
<p>Consistent shingling requires consistent bag dimensions, consistent bag spacing before the shingling section, and a clean speed transition between the fast and slow belts. Bags with variable fill levels produce inconsistent shingle patterns, an overfilled bag sits higher and shingles differently than an underfilled bag. The speed transition zone (where the two belts meet) must be as short as possible, a gap between belts allows bags to drop and lose their position, disrupting the shingle pattern.</p>
<p>Overhead hold-down belts above the shingling section apply gentle downward pressure to keep bags flat during the overlap process. Without hold-down, the trailing edge of the shingled bag tends to lift and curl upward instead of lying flat over the leading bag. Hold-down belt pressure must be light enough to avoid compressing the bag contents (which changes bag dimension and can damage product) while firm enough to maintain the shingle.</p>
<h2 id="stack-heading">Bag Stacking</h2>
<p>Bag stacking creates vertical stacks of bags for case packing. A case pack pattern of "2 stacks of 6 bags" requires the bag loading system to count bags into groups of 6, stack each group vertically, and present two stacks side-by-side for insertion into the case. <a href="/conveyor-functions/stacking-machines/">Stacking machines</a> use several methods to build bag stacks from a single-file stream.</p>
<h3>Drop Stacking</h3>
<p>Drop stacking feeds bags off the end of a conveyor and drops them onto a platform or lower conveyor. Each bag falls on top of the previous one, building a stack from the bottom up. A photoeye or product counter tracks the bag count. When the stack reaches the target count, the platform indexes forward to clear space for the next stack. Drop stacking is simple and reliable for flat, stable bags (pillow bags, flat pouches) but produces inconsistent stacks with stand-up pouches or irregularly shaped bags that land in unpredictable orientations.</p>
<p>Drop height must be minimized because bags that fall far shift position, bounce, and land misaligned. Short drops keep stacks neat. Adjustable-height platforms that lower as the stack grows maintain a constant drop distance for every bag in the stack.</p>
<h3>Flighted Stacking</h3>
<p>Flighted stacking uses an inclined conveyor with flights (cleats or paddles) that push bags upward along an inclined surface. Bags accumulate against a stop plate at the top of the incline, stacking face-to-face in a controlled manner. A gate or pusher releases the completed stack into the case packer infeed. Flighted stacking produces consistent, tight stacks and handles a wider range of bag types than drop stacking.</p>
<p>Flight spacing sets the maximum bag thickness the system can handle. Changeover between bag sizes requires adjusting flight spacing, some flighted stackers use quick-change flight attachment systems for fast changeover. Flighted stacking systems are typically integrated directly with case packers for synchronized bag-to-case loading.</p>
<h2 id="bib-heading">Bag-in-Box Loading</h2>
<p>Bag-in-box loading places bags into cases (corrugated boxes) in a specified arrangement, flat stacks, on-edge rows, shingled layers, or a combination. The case pack pattern determines how bags must be oriented and grouped before insertion. A 24-count snack bag case might use 4 layers of 6 bags stacked flat, or 2 rows of 12 bags standing on edge. Each pattern requires different bag handling upstream of the <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packer</a>.</p>
<h3>Flat Loading</h3>
<p>Flat loading lays bags horizontally into the case in stacked layers. The bag loading conveyor delivers bags flat (face up or face down), counts them into groups matching the layer count, and presents each group for insertion. A top-loading case packer lowers a vacuum head or gripper to pick the bag group and place it into the open case. Flat loading produces stable, cube-efficient case packs and works well for thin, flat bags (single-serve snack bags, flat pouches, sachets).</p>
<p>Layer separation, a cardboard divider or slip sheet between bag layers, prevents bags from interlocking between layers and eases retail display unpacking. The bag loading system pauses between layers for the case packer to insert a divider before loading the next bag layer.</p>
<h3>On-Edge Loading</h3>
<p>On-edge loading stands bags upright in the case with the bag face visible from the case opening. This arrangement suits retail display cases where the consumer sees the bag graphics through an open case top. <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Collating and laning systems</a> arrange bags side-by-side in the correct number of lanes matching the case width. The on-edge row slides or pushes into the case from the side or end.</p>
<p>On-edge loading requires bags with enough structural rigidity to stand without toppling. Bags filled with granular product (chips, cookies, cereal) have a settled lower section that provides a base, but the upper portion may flop over. Side compression plates hold bags upright during the loading sequence. Stand-up pouches with bottom gussets load on-edge more reliably than pillow bags because the gusset provides a flat bottom for standing.</p>
<h3>Pouch Handling Specifics</h3>
<p>Stand-up pouches, spouted pouches, and retort pouches each present unique loading challenges. Spouted pouches have a rigid spout that protrudes above the pouch body, the spout must align consistently for case packing and cannot be compressed during stacking. Retort pouches (sterilized food pouches) are thick, stiff after processing, and resist bending, they stack well but their rigidity limits how tightly they pack into cases. Spouted pouch loading systems use spout-locating guides that align the spout position during conveying and loading, ensuring the spout does not interfere with case packer operation.</p>
<h2 id="slug-heading">Bag Feeding for Case Packers</h2>
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/slug-loading/">Slug loading</a> groups bags into a counted row (slug) and loads the entire slug into the case in one motion. The bag loading conveyor meters bags into groups matching the case row count, closes the gap between bags in the slug, and presents the slug to the case packer's loading mechanism (pusher bar, bucket, or robotic arm).</p>
<p>Bag slug formation is more challenging than rigid product slug formation. Rigid cartons pushed together in a slug maintain alignment, their flat sides contact each other cleanly. Bags pushed together in a slug deform, bunch, fold at the edges, and shift out of alignment. Gentle slug formation, using low-pressure conveyor compression rather than hard mechanical pushers, prevents bag deformation during slug building.</p>
<p>Servo-driven slug belts control each bag's position during slug formation. A multi-zone servo belt feeds bags one at a time, decelerates each bag as it contacts the previous bag in the slug, and positions the completed slug at the case packer loading point. The servo system applies minimal compression force, just enough to close gaps without deforming the bags. Servo-driven bag slug formation is typically integrated directly with the case packing system for coordinated motion control.</p>
<h3>Robotic Bag Loading</h3>
<p>Delta robots and SCARA robots pick individual bags or bag groups from the conveyor and place them directly into cases. Robotic loading handles the widest range of bag types, orientations, and case patterns because the robot arm can grasp, orient, and place each bag individually. Vision systems guide the robot to each bag's position on the conveyor, compensating for the position variation inherent in flexible product conveying.</p>
<p>Robotic bag loading uses vacuum end-of-arm tooling (EOAT) with suction cups sized and positioned for the specific bag dimensions. The vacuum cups contact the bag's flat face and lift it without squeezing or compressing the bag body. Bag film surface quality affects vacuum grip, matte films grip better than glossy laminated films. Porous or perforated bags (for venting gas from fresh produce) require foam-faced vacuum cups that seal against the rough surface. Robot cells need guarding: OSHA's <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.212</a> sets general machine guarding requirements.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">Line Integration</h2>
<p>A complete bag loading line runs from the form-fill-seal (FFS) machine or premade pouch filler through conveying, inspection, and loading into cases. The typical sequence is: bag machine discharge → takeaway conveyor → checkweigher → metal detector → reject diverter → accumulation conveyor → metering conveyor → stacking or shingling section → case packer infeed. Each section must handle the bag gently while maintaining the throughput rate set by the bag machine.</p>
<p>Bag machines (vertical FFS, horizontal FFS, premade pouch fillers) discharge bags at rates that vary widely with machine type, bag size, and product. Use the bagger's rated and planned peak speed as the design rate for everything downstream. The bag loading system must keep pace with the machine output. Multiple bag machines feeding a single case packer require a merge conveyor that combines the streams from multiple machines into one line without bag damage or orientation loss.</p>
<h3>Accumulation for Bags</h3>
<p>Bag accumulation between the bag machine and the case packer buffers production during case packer stoppages (case supply interruptions, case sealer jams, pallet changes). Standard conveyor accumulation techniques, zero-pressure zones, low-backpressure belts, apply to bags with modifications. Bags cannot tolerate the backpressure that rigid products withstand. Excessive backpressure deforms bags, pops seals, and damages product. Low-pressure accumulation conveyors use controlled zones that stop each bag independently without transmitting pressure from downstream bags to upstream bags. Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2010_624_ezlogiccomponent.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EZLogic manual</a> describes this zero-pressure principle as stopping product "without the buildup of line pressure."</p>
<p>Vertical accumulation systems store bags in a buffer tower using an elevator that lifts bags from the main conveyor to storage shelves and releases them back onto the conveyor when the downstream machine resumes. Vertical accumulators provide minutes of buffer capacity in a small floor footprint, important in facilities where floor space between the bag machine and case packer is limited. For broader context on accumulation and buffering, see <a href="/conveyor-functions/storage-conveyors/">storage conveyors</a>.</p>
<h3>Changeover and Flexibility</h3>
<p>Bag packaging lines run multiple bag sizes and styles. A snack food line might run 1-ounce single-serve bags, 10-ounce family size bags, and 16-ounce party size bags on the same case packer. Each bag size requires different guide rail widths, stacking heights, case patterns, and metering speeds. Quick-changeover bag loading systems use tool-free guide rail adjustment (hand knobs or quick-release clamps), recipe-based servo settings (stored electronically for each SKU), and modular stacking components that swap for different stack heights. Set a target changeover time in the spec and ask suppliers to demonstrate it, since changeover time drives line utilization on short runs.</p>
